frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-125-  { text: `The gray rock method is a defense strategy against narcissists and manipulators. You become as boring and unresponsive as a gray rock — short answers, no emotional reactions, no interesting stories. Narcissists feed on drama and emotional responses. When you become bland, you starve them. They lose interest and move on to more reactive targets. The technique works because manipulators don't actually want you — they want your emotional energy. Remove the supply, and the predator finds easier prey. It is the psychological equivalent of playing dead.`, type: 'Dark Psychology', styles: ['dark_psychology', 'minimal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-126-  { text: `There is a manipulation pattern called DARVO — Deny, Attack, Reverse Victim and Offender. When confronted with their behavior, the manipulator first denies everything. Then they attack the person confronting them. Finally, they reverse the roles — claiming they are the real victim and you are the aggressor. "I never said that. Why are you always attacking me? You're the one being abusive." It is devastatingly effective because it forces the accuser to defend themselves instead of pressing the original issue. The conversation shifts from their behavior to your character. By the time you realize what happened, the original issue has evaporated.`, type: 'Dark Psychology', styles: ['dark_psychology', 'noir'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-127-  { text: `The concept of "flying monkeys" comes from The Wizard of Oz, but psychologists use it to describe people a narcissist recruits to do their bidding. The narcissist never confronts you directly — instead, they tell their version of events to friends, family, and coworkers, who then pressure you on the narcissist's behalf. The flying monkeys believe they're helping. They don't realize they're being weaponized. The narcissist maintains clean hands while others deliver the punishment. Isolation is the goal — one by one, your support network is turned into surveillance.`, type: 'Dark Psychology', styles: ['dark_psychology', 'dark_horror'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-128-  { text: `Emotional vampires are not a metaphor in psychology — they're a clinical observation. Certain personality types systematically drain the emotional energy of everyone around them. They dominate conversations, manufacture crises, and create a gravitational pull of need that leaves you exhausted after every interaction. The five types identified by psychiatrist Albert Bernstein are the narcissist, the antisocial, the histrionic, the obsessive-compulsive, and the paranoid. The common thread is that they take more emotional energy than they give, and they do it so consistently that the people around them burn out without understanding why.`, type: 'Dark Psychology', styles: ['dark_psychology', 'gothic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-129-  { text: `The city of Constantinople was conquered in fourteen fifty-three partly because of a single unlocked gate. The Ottoman army had besieged the city for fifty-three days with the largest cannon ever built, but the walls held. Then someone discovered that a small postern gate called the Kerkoporta had been left open — possibly by accident, possibly by treachery. A handful of Ottoman soldiers slipped through, raised their flag on the inner wall, and the defenders panicked. The greatest fortified city in history, which had withstood twenty-three sieges over a thousand years, fell because of one door.`, type: 'History', styles: ['war_military', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:130:  { text: `There is a radio signal from space called the fast radio burst that releases more energy in a millisecond than the sun produces in an entire day. The first one was detected in two thousand seven in archived data from two thousand one. They come from billions of light-years away, last only a few milliseconds, and most never repeat. In twenty twenty, scientists traced one to a magnetar — a neutron star with a magnetic field a quadrillion times stronger than Earth's. But many bursts remain unexplained. Something out there is screaming across the universe in millisecond bursts of impossible energy, and we are only beginning to hear it.`, type: 'Space', styles: ['sci_fi', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-131-  { text: `There are more bacteria in your mouth right now than there are people on Earth. Roughly seven hundred species of microbes colonize the human oral cavity, and at any given moment, between one and ten billion individual bacteria inhabit your mouth. After you brush your teeth, the population drops to about one hundred million — and recovers within hours. Your mouth is one of the most biodiverse ecosystems on the planet. Some of these bacteria protect you. Others, left unchecked, will dissolve your teeth from the inside out. You are hosting a civilization and a war simultaneously.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['documentary', 'nature_doc'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-132-  { text: `There is a place in Turkmenistan called the Door to Hell — a crater seventy meters wide that has been burning continuously since nineteen seventy-one. Soviet geologists were drilling for natural gas when the ground collapsed, creating a massive sinkhole filled with methane. They set it on fire, expecting it to burn out in a few weeks. It has been burning for over fifty years. The crater glows orange at night and can be seen from miles away in the desert. In twenty twenty-two, the Turkmen president ordered it extinguished for environmental reasons. It is still burning.`, type: 'Curiosity', styles: ['cinematic', 'dark_horror'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-133-  { text: `In twenty nineteen, scientists captured the first photograph of a black hole — a supermassive object at the center of galaxy Messier eighty-seven, fifty-five million light-years from Earth. The image required a telescope the size of the planet — eight radio observatories on four continents synchronized to the precision of a billionth of a second, collecting five petabytes of data that took two years to process. The orange ring in the image is not the black hole itself — it is light bent around the event horizon, the boundary beyond which nothing escapes. You are looking at the edge of the unknowable.`, type: 'Space', styles: ['sci_fi', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-134-  { text: `Tardigrades are microscopic animals that can survive conditions that would destroy almost any other organism. They have been revived after being frozen for thirty years at minus two hundred seventy-two degrees Celsius — one degree above absolute zero. They survive boiling water, pressures six times greater than the deepest ocean trench, radiation hundreds of times the lethal dose for humans, and the vacuum of space. In twenty nineteen, a lunar lander crashed on the moon carrying dehydrated tardigrades. Scientists believe they may still be intact on the lunar surface, waiting. They have survived all five mass extinctions on Earth. They will almost certainly survive us.`, type: 'Nature', styles: ['nature_doc', 'sci_fi'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-135-  { text: `Dark matter makes up roughly twenty-seven percent of the universe and dark energy makes up sixty-eight percent. Ordinary matter — everything you can see, touch, and measure, including every star, planet, and person — accounts for less than five percent. We are the rounding error. The vast majority of the universe is made of something we cannot detect, cannot explain, and have never directly observed. We know dark matter exists because galaxies spin too fast to be held together by visible matter alone. Something invisible is providing the gravity. The universe is mostly made of a substance we named but do not understand.`, type: 'Space', styles: ['sci_fi', 'surreal'] },
--
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-244-  { text: `I work the night shift at a gas station in the middle of nowhere. Last Thursday around three a.m., a woman came in wearing a wedding dress covered in mud. She bought a pack of gum, a road atlas, and a prepaid phone. She paid in exact change, smiled at me like we were old friends, and said, "Don't ever let anyone plan your life for you." Then she walked into the dark. I watched her taillights disappear down the highway. I still think about her twice a week. Whoever she was, I hope she found where she was going.`, type: 'Anecdote', styles: ['reddit_story', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-245-  { text: `My grandfather never talked about the war. When he died, we found a shoe box in his closet with seventy-three letters he had written to a woman named Estelle — all unsent. The letters described everything: the mud, the cold, the sounds at night, the friend he couldn't save. The last letter, dated nineteen forty-six, said, "I made it home, but I left the best part of me over there. I'm sorry I can't be who you remember." We found Estelle. She was ninety-one, living in a nursing home in Vermont. She said she had waited for those letters her entire life.`, type: 'Anecdote', styles: ['reddit_story', 'vintage_retro'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-246-  { text: `My neighbor has been mowing a perfect spiral pattern into his lawn for the last eight months. It started as a simple circle and has expanded outward, one pass per week, into a massive geometric spiral visible on satellite imagery. I finally asked him about it. He said his daughter is studying abroad in South Korea and she told him she misses looking out airplane windows. "When she flies home for Christmas," he said, "I want her to see something from the sky and know she's almost home." The spiral is two hundred feet across now. He has not missed a single week.`, type: 'Anecdote', styles: ['reddit_story', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-247-  { text: `In twenty twenty-three, a deepfake of a CEO authorized a twenty-five-million-dollar wire transfer during a video call. The finance officer saw his boss's face, heard his boss's voice, and followed instructions that seemed perfectly routine. The real CEO was asleep in another time zone. By the time anyone noticed, the money had been laundered through seventeen shell companies across four continents. The technology that made this possible costs less than fifty dollars and runs on a consumer laptop. The future of fraud is not about breaking into vaults. It is about wearing someone else's face.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['cyberpunk', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-248-  { text: `In Shenzhen, China, there is a market where you can buy a fully functional smartphone for twelve dollars. It will have a camera, internet access, and apps. It will also contain firmware that sends your location, contacts, and browsing data to servers you will never know about. Fourteen-year-old engineers disassemble and reassemble circuit boards in workshops the size of closets, building devices that connect a billion people to a network that watches them back. The most connected generation in history carries surveillance equipment voluntarily, in their pockets, and calls it convenience.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['cyberpunk', 'documentary'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js:249:  { text: `Neuralink implanted its first brain chip in a human patient in twenty twenty-four. The patient, a quadriplegic, learned to control a computer cursor with his thoughts within weeks. He played chess online using only his mind. The technology promises to restore movement to the paralyzed, sight to the blind, and memory to the forgetful. It also means a corporation now has direct read access to human neural activity. The line between healing the brain and reading the brain is measured in software updates. Every medical miracle in this century will come with a terms of service agreement.`, type: 'Science', styles: ['cyberpunk', 'sci_fi'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-250-  { text: `In nineteen thirty-eight, two teenagers from Cleveland sold the rights to Superman for one hundred thirty dollars. Jerry Siegel and Joe Shuster had spent years being rejected by every publisher in the country before Detective Comics finally said yes. Superman became the most profitable fictional character in history, generating billions of dollars across comics, films, and merchandise. Siegel and Shuster spent decades in poverty, fighting legal battles for recognition. They didn't receive credit on the comics until nineteen seventy-five, and only after threatening to go public during production of the first Superman film. The men who created the most powerful hero in fiction were powerless to claim him.`, type: 'History', styles: ['comic_book', 'vintage_retro'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-251-  { text: `Jack Kirby drew over twenty thousand pages of comic book art in his career, creating or co-creating Captain America, the Fantastic Four, the X-Men, the New Gods, and dozens of other characters worth billions. He worked at a drafting table in his basement, often drawing through the night, smoking cigars and listening to the radio. Marvel paid him a page rate. He never received royalties. When he asked for his original artwork back — thousands of pages worth — Marvel told him they had lost most of it. The man who drew the modern mythology of America died in nineteen ninety-four. The characters he created will outlive the company that underpaid him.`, type: 'History', styles: ['comic_book', 'cinematic'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-252-  { text: `The Comics Code Authority, established in nineteen fifty-four, banned horror, crime, and any content deemed unwholesome from American comic books for nearly fifty years. It was created in response to psychiatrist Fredric Wertham's book "Seduction of the Innocent," which claimed comics caused juvenile delinquency. Overnight, hundreds of horror and crime titles were canceled. EC Comics, publisher of Tales from the Crypt and MAD, nearly went bankrupt. The Code banned the words "horror" and "terror" from covers, prohibited sympathy for criminals, and required all authority figures to be portrayed positively. An entire art form was lobotomized by moral panic.`, type: 'History', styles: ['comic_book', 'dark_academia'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-253-  { text: `There is a dead mall in Ohio where the fountains still run. The anchor stores closed in two thousand eight. The food court shut down in twenty twelve. The last tenant, a phone repair kiosk, left in twenty seventeen. But the building management company continues to pay the water bill, and the fountains in the central atrium cycle endlessly, their pumps humming beneath music that no longer plays through speakers that still hang from the ceiling. Security cameras record empty corridors. Escalators carry no one between floors. The mall exists now as pure architecture — a cathedral of commerce with no congregation, maintained by an algorithm that never received the order to stop.`, type: 'Curiosity', styles: ['vaporwave', 'surreal'] },
frontend\src\shared\data\stories.js-254-  { text: `Muzak, the company that invented elevator music, was originally designed as a productivity tool. In the nineteen forties, studies showed that workers on factory floors moved faster when background music followed a specific tempo curve — slow at the start of a shift, faster toward the middle, slow again at the end. The company called this "stimulus progression." By the nineteen eighties, Muzak soundtracks played in over one hundred million square feet of commercial space. You have spent hours of your life being emotionally manipulated by invisible music engineered to make you buy things slightly faster. The company went bankrupt in two thousand nine. Its patents were acquired by a mood-engineering firm.`, type: 'Curiosity', styles: ['vaporwave', 'vintage_retro'] },
--
